      <ul><li><strong>SMD rework and multi-pin parts:</strong> lifting a chip with many pins is a job for hot air, not a bigger iron. Our <a href="/guides/smd-rework-hot-air-and-reflow">SMD rework guide</a> explains hot air and reflow. See also <a href="/guides/through-hole-vs-surface-mount-soldering">through-hole vs surface-mount soldering</a>.</li><li><strong>Removing parts:</strong> technique and the right tool beat raw power. See <a href="/guides/how-to-desolder">how to desolder</a>.</li><li><strong>Field, car and away-from-the-bench work:</strong> a battery iron fits better. Our <a href="/best-cordless-soldering-irons">cordless roundup</a> covers those.</li><li><strong>Joints that still look dull or lumpy:</strong> that's usually practice, not hardware. A board from our <a href="/best-soldering-practice-kits">soldering practice kits</a> guide and <a href="/guides/how-to-fix-a-cold-solder-joint">how to fix a cold solder joint</a> will do more than a new iron.</li></ul>
